<wpf:ResourceDictionary xml:space="preserve"
                        xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
                        xmlns:s="clr-namespace:System;assembly=mscorlib"
                        xmlns:ss="urn:shemas-jetbrains-com:settings-storage-xaml"
                        xmlns:wpf="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation">

  <!--
    ReSharper inspection severities for the Qodana Community for .NET scan (see qodana.yaml).

    This file exists because that linter reads settings from here and nowhere else: per JetBrains'
    own documentation, "the Qodana Community for .NET linter does not support profiles and their
    configuration options", and it picks up ".DotSettings" files instead. Two earlier attempts to
    express these decisions in qodana.yaml - a top-level `exclude:` list, then the documented
    `profile.base` + `inspections` form - were both accepted without complaint and silently ignored:
    the runs on 98a3c95 and 6660580 still reported every finding they were meant to drop.

    Every entry below is an inspection whose finding is wrong for this codebase, with the reason
    recorded. Nothing is turned off to make a number go down; a finding that is merely tedious to fix
    is fixed in the code instead. Inspection ids encode "." as "_002E".
  -->

  <!--
    Record positional properties that only a serializer ever reads.

    These fire on the JSON wire contracts: ProviderAdminModels.cs alone is 37 records behind
    ProviderAdminService/UsageAdminService, and LocalEndpointResponder's records are the /v1/models,
    /api/tags and /api/show payloads. Their properties are constructed in C# and then read by
    System.Text.Json through reflection, which the inspection cannot see, so it reports every wire
    field as unused. Acting on it would delete the fields and silently break the API.
  -->
  <s:String x:Key="/Default/CodeInspection/Highlighting/InspectionSeverities/=NotAccessedPositionalProperty_002EGlobal/@EntryIndexedValue">DO_NOT_SHOW</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="/Default/CodeInspection/Highlighting/InspectionSeverities/=NotAccessedPositionalProperty_002ELocal/@EntryIndexedValue">DO_NOT_SHOW</s:String>

  <!--
    "This property needs no setter."

    All 13 sites are places where the writer is not C# code the analyzer can follow:
      - 11 are options classes bound with configuration.GetSection(...).Bind(options). The binder
        assigns through the property setter by reflection, so removing the setter does not fail the
        build - it makes the option silently keep its default and ignore appsettings.json, which no
        test would catch.
      - 2 are DialectDelimiter's validating overrides of a positional record's properties. That
        file's own comment records why "init" is load-bearing there: "with" copies set "init"
        properties directly, bypassing the field initializer that performs the validation.

    PropertyCanBeMadeInitOnly stays enabled and has been applied - "init" keeps a setter, so binding
    and System.Text.Json both still work; only removing the setter breaks them.
  -->
  <s:String x:Key="/Default/CodeInspection/Highlighting/InspectionSeverities/=AutoPropertyCanBeMadeGetOnly_002EGlobal/@EntryIndexedValue">DO_NOT_SHOW</s:String>

  <!--
    "This public member/type is unused" leftovers. Phase P9 put the WASM GUI (Gui.Web, Gui.Components,
    Gui.Admin, Gui.Telemetry, Gui.Charts, Gui.Console) into TotallyHotArcRouter.Qodana.slnx, so the
    previous "GUI is absent from the Linux scan" justification no longer holds. MemberCanBePrivate,
    UnusedMember, UnusedMemberInSuper, UnusedType, and EventNeverSubscribedTo are therefore re-enabled.

    Exactly one blind spot survives the re-enable: Tray (WinForms, net10.0-windows) is still omitted from
    this scan, so a member whose only caller is Tray reads as unused. SessionRouterConnector and
    RouterConnectionSupervisor.Provider are the two that do today; both carry a
    `// ReSharper disable once ...` and a <remarks> naming the reason, so the judgement is recorded at the
    member rather than re-made on every scan.

    .razor markup is NOT a blind spot - this was checked rather than assumed. ToastService.Changed,
    ClusterModelAdminStore.Changed and six sibling store events are subscribed only from inline @code in
    .razor files, and the scan reports none of them. So an unused/unsubscribed finding on a
    Gui.Components member is a real finding about that member, not an artifact of the linter skipping
    components. Do not wave one away on "a component probably uses it".

    The compiler is the arbiter of the remaining cases. Reducing a member to private - or deleting it -
    and getting a clean build of TotallyHotArcRouter.slnx, which DOES include Tray and DOES compile Razor,
    settles whether it really had a caller. That is how all 48 MemberCanBePrivate findings were confirmed.

    UnusedMemberInSuper is not a dead-code inspection and should not be read as one. It fires when every
    call goes through the concrete type instead of the interface, so the member is live and the only
    question is whether the interface should still declare it - a design change, which needs a reason
    beyond the scan. Every current instance is suppressed at its declaration with a <remarks> saying so,
    rather than solution-wide here, so a genuinely new one still gets reported and read on its merits.
    EventNeverSubscribedTo has the same two shapes and is handled the same way: "only implementations of
    event X are subscribed to" is the interface-vs-concrete case and is suppressed at the member, while a
    bare "event X is never subscribed to" is a real finding - it caught three raised-but-ignored events.

    Still suppressed because the scan still cannot see these callers:

      - ClassNeverInstantiated: DI (AddSingleton/AddHostedService) and System.Text.Json
        ReadFromJsonAsync&lt;T&gt; targets.
      - UnusedAutoPropertyAccessor: options classes bound via configuration.GetSection().Bind and
        JSON wire DTOs whose accessors are used only by reflection.
      - UnusedMethodReturnValue.Global: fluent IServiceCollection returns (see the UnusedParameter
        block below, which already covers the Local twin).
  -->
  <s:String x:Key="/Default/CodeInspection/Highlighting/InspectionSeverities/=ClassNeverInstantiated_002EGlobal/@EntryIndexedValue">DO_NOT_SHOW</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="/Default/CodeInspection/Highlighting/InspectionSeverities/=UnusedMethodReturnValue_002EGlobal/@EntryIndexedValue">DO_NOT_SHOW</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="/Default/CodeInspection/Highlighting/InspectionSeverities/=UnusedAutoPropertyAccessor_002EGlobal/@EntryIndexedValue">DO_NOT_SHOW</s:String>

  <!--
    Abbreviations. "AI" is load-bearing, not cosmetic: ProviderType.OpenAI and ProviderType.AzureOpenAI
    are persisted by member name - ProviderTemplate.DisplayName's remarks record that the display label
    is deliberately separate from the stored value - so taking the rename to OpenAi/AzureOpenAi would
    silently invalidate every provider configuration already on disk. "BE" is big-endian, in
    BedrockProviderTests' WriteUInt32BE helper.
  -->
  <s:Boolean x:Key="/Default/CodeStyle/Naming/CSharpNaming/Abbreviations/=AI/@EntryIndexedValue">True</s:Boolean>
  <s:Boolean x:Key="/Default/CodeStyle/Naming/CSharpNaming/Abbreviations/=BE/@EntryIndexedValue">True</s:Boolean>

  <!--
    "This method has an async overload with cancellation support."

    All three are SqliteCommand.ExecuteReader inside OodBootstrapSampleSource and
    OodClusterBootstrapSampleSource. Microsoft.Data.Sqlite's async surface is synchronous underneath
    - SQLite has no async I/O, so ExecuteReaderAsync forwards to ExecuteReader and returns a
    completed Task. Adopting it here would add a state machine and a Task per row-set to a
    corpus-loading loop while releasing no thread and honouring no cancellation the synchronous call
    does not already honour, since it never yields.

    These are solution-wide rather than test-scoped because the sites are production.
  -->
  <s:String x:Key="/Default/CodeInspection/Highlighting/InspectionSeverities/=MethodHasAsyncOverloadWithCancellation/@EntryIndexedValue">DO_NOT_SHOW</s:String>

  <!--
    "This if/else can be a ?: inside one call."

    StartupHealthCheckHostedService's only site logs one of two messages depending on whether the
    pricing database already existed. Taking the advice means selecting the message template with a
    ternary inside the LogInformation call - and AGENTS.md requires the template to stay a static
    string literal, never interpolated or computed, so structured-logging tools can group on it. The
    inspection is asking for exactly what that rule forbids.
  -->
  <s:String x:Key="/Default/CodeInspection/Highlighting/InspectionSeverities/=ConvertIfStatementToConditionalTernaryExpression/@EntryIndexedValue">DO_NOT_SHOW</s:String>

  <!--
    "This private field is always assigned before use and can be a local."

    All three are readonly fields on RequestInterceptor, assigned once in its constructor. AGENTS.md
    names RequestInterceptor as one of three hubs whose edits need a CodeGraph blast-radius pass
    first, and there is nothing here to buy with that: converting a constructor-injected readonly
    dependency into a local would mean constructing it per call instead.
  -->
  <s:String x:Key="/Default/CodeInspection/Highlighting/InspectionSeverities/=PrivateFieldCanBeConvertedToLocalVariable/@EntryIndexedValue">DO_NOT_SHOW</s:String>

  <!--
    "This parameter is unused / its return value is never used / this switch arm is redundant."

    Three families that are each right about the code and wrong about what to do:

      - UnusedParameter: ProxyMiddleware.InvokeCoreAsync's `next` is the middleware shape;
        TelemetryPublisher's and SpendTracker's `cancellationToken` and IDimensionInferrer's
        `language` are interface contracts, and "no current implementation uses it" is not a
        statement about the API.
      - UnusedMethodReturnValue: AddUpdate, AddQualityAndObservability and AddBackgroundServices
        return IServiceCollection because that is the fluent DI convention, whether or not today's
        caller chains from it.
      - RedundantSwitchExpressionArms: an explicit `MetricRange.AllTime => DateTimeOffset.MinValue`
        alongside the default documents that AllTime is deliberately unbounded rather than falling
        through by accident.
  -->
  <s:String x:Key="/Default/CodeInspection/Highlighting/InspectionSeverities/=UnusedParameter_002EGlobal/@EntryIndexedValue">DO_NOT_SHOW</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="/Default/CodeInspection/Highlighting/InspectionSeverities/=UnusedParameter_002ELocal/@EntryIndexedValue">DO_NOT_SHOW</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="/Default/CodeInspection/Highlighting/InspectionSeverities/=UnusedMethodReturnValue_002ELocal/@EntryIndexedValue">DO_NOT_SHOW</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="/Default/CodeInspection/Highlighting/InspectionSeverities/=RedundantSwitchExpressionArms/@EntryIndexedValue">DO_NOT_SHOW</s:String>

  <!--
    "Replace this backing field with the `field` keyword."

    DeferredTelemetryPublisher's _resolved cache. Declined for the same reason the `with` element was
    reverted on this branch: `field` is brand-new syntax whose payoff is a saved declaration line,
    and the cost is a reader who has to know it. Revisit when it is unremarkable.
  -->
  <s:String x:Key="/Default/CodeInspection/Highlighting/InspectionSeverities/=ReplaceWithFieldKeyword/@EntryIndexedValue">DO_NOT_SHOW</s:String>

  <!--
    "Simplify this string interpolation."

    LogLineFormatter's only site would inline the named TimestampFormat constant as a literal format
    specifier, because a specifier cannot reference a constant. Trading a named constant for a magic
    string inside an interpolation is not a simplification.
  -->
  <s:String x:Key="/Default/CodeInspection/Highlighting/InspectionSeverities/=SimplifyStringInterpolation/@EntryIndexedValue">DO_NOT_SHOW</s:String>

</wpf:ResourceDictionary>
